  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Nonobstructive coronary artery disease (I25.10)</content>. CAD-RADS 2 with a calcium score of 410: coronary atherosclerosis without a narrowing that limits blood flow, which fits his brief, infrequent exertional tightness. The 2023 chronic coronary disease guideline calls for a high-intensity statin, low-dose aspirin, blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg and stopping smoking; no functional testing is needed while symptoms stay infrequent. [S]<list><item>Atorvastatin 40 mg daily, with a lipid panel and ALT in about eight weeks.</item><item>Aspirin 81 mg daily.</item><item>Walk daily at a pace that allows talking; call 911 for chest pain at rest or lasting more than fifteen minutes.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tobacco use disorder (F17.290)</content>. Ready to quit after seeing his CT. Varenicline is the most effective single medicine and is safe in stable coronary disease; counseling adds to it. [S]<list><item>Varenicline 0.5 mg daily for three days, then twice daily, then 1 mg twice daily from day eight, for twelve weeks.</item><item>Quit date in the second week.</item><item>Counseling at each visit and the state quitline.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gout (M10.9)</content>. Urate 8.6 mg/dL with two flares in the past year: urate-lowering therapy is strongly recommended by ACR 2020. eGFR 74. HLA-B*58:01 testing is not indicated by his ancestry. [S]<list><item>Allopurinol 100 mg daily, raised by 100 mg every two to five weeks to a urate below 6 mg/dL.</item><item>Colchicine 0.6 mg daily for three to six months to prevent flares.</item><item>Urate in three to four weeks.</item><item>If a flare comes, keep taking allopurinol and call.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prolonged grief disorder (F43.81)</content>. PG-13-R 37 and PHQ-9 9 after four sessions. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of death for two weeks. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly grief therapy; telephone sessions while he travels in August.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Grief severity (PG-13-R) and the DSM-5-TR prolonged grief disorder criteria</td><td>At diagnosis and every four weeks during therapy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PG-13-R 37</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Prolonged grief disorder therapy: sessions attended</td><td>Weekly for sixteen weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Four of four sessions attended</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression (PHQ-9), to watch for major depression</td><td>At baseline and every four weeks during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 9, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk review, safety plan, means safety and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of death for two weeks; safety plan kept</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep, without sedative-hypnotics</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About five hours</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Social contact and his writing</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two visits with friends; started writing again for an hour most mornings</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Tobacco use (cigars a day), readiness and counseling</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About eight cigars a day; ready to quit; varenicline started</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Varenicline: adherence, nausea, sleep and mood changes</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Starting today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Chest symptoms on exertion</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One episode three weeks ago on a steep hill; none at rest</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>CT coronary angiography for stable exertional chest tightness</td><td>Once, at evaluation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Calcium score 410; CAD-RADS 2 (25 to 49 percent proximal left anterior descending); no obstructive disease</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>136/80 mmHg; target below 130/80</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel and ALT on a statin</td><td>4 to 12 weeks after starting, then every 3 to 12 months</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Baseline LDL 150 mg/dL and ALT 24 U/L at intake; repeat in about eight weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Serum urate during allopurinol titration</td><td>Every 2 to 5 weeks while titrating, then every 6 months</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Baseline 8.6 mg/dL; repeat three to four weeks after starting allopurinol</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Gout flares and colchicine prophylaxis</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>A one-day twinge last week; colchicine started with allopurinol</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>PG-13-R below 30 and back to writing and seeing friends. In progress; target 2026-10-16 [S]</item><item>No thoughts of death and a safety plan he would use. In progress; target 2026-09-11 [S]</item><item>Stop smoking cigars. In progress; target 2026-10-16 [S]</item><item>LDL cholesterol at least 50 percent lower on a high-intensity statin. In progress; target 2026-09-11 [S]</item><item>Serum urate below 6 mg/dL and no gout flares. In progress; target 2026-10-16 [S]</item><item>Six or more hours of sleep on most nights without a sleeping pill. In progress; target 2026-10-16 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Prolonged Grief Disorder (DSM-5-TR criteria, patient and family information) (2022); Validation of the new DSM-5-TR criteria for prolonged grief disorder and the PG-13-Revised (PG-13-R) scale (2021); Treatment of complicated grief: a randomized controlled trial (2005); Optimizing Treatment of Complicated Grief: A Randomized Clinical Trial (2016); Loss of partner and suicide risks among oldest old: a population-based register study (2004); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults, Including Pregnant Persons: Interventions (2021); 2021 AHA/ACC/ASE/CHEST/SAEM/SCCT/SCMR Guideline for the Evaluation and Diagnosis of Chest Pain (2021); 2023 AHA/ACC/ACCP/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease (2023); 2020 American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Management of Gout (2020); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, ischemic heart disease, hyperlipidemia, and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Telephone check in two weeks. Urate blood test in three to four weeks. Thirteen-week review in September, after his August trip. Call the clinic or 988 at any time. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-11: thirteen-week review: grief therapy, smoking, a gout flare while away, the urate and lipid results. [S]</paragraph></text>

